is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The helpdesk ticket claims that PC5 (172.16.10.5, VLAN 10) cannot reach any host in the 172.16.30.x range (VLAN 30, design group). Exhaustive live verification shows this reported symptom does not exist in the current network. Inter-VLAN routing between VLAN 10 and VLAN 30 is fully operational in every measurable way.

The inter-VLAN routing device is mswitch0, a Linux multilayer switch that bridges VLANs 10, 20, and 30 via dedicated SVI sub-interfaces (vlan10@br0 at 172.16.10.254, vlan30@br0 at 172.16.30.254), both in the UP state. The kernel IP forwarding flag is set to 1 on the box and on all VLAN interfaces. Access switch1 correctly VLAN-tags VLAN-10 traffic on its uplink to mswitch0 (via the eth0.10 sub-interface in br_vlan10), and switch3 correctly VLAN-tags VLAN-30 traffic (via eth0.30 in br_vlan30). No iptables or ebtables rules exist on mswitch0 that would block forwarded traffic. ARP is resolved for all hosts in both subnets. Direct pings from pc5 to all three design-group machines (172.16.30.12, .13, .14) return 0% packet loss, as does the reverse path from pc12 to pc5. Both VLAN gateways (.10.254 and .30.254) are reachable from pc5 directly.

The ticket describes a fault that does not exist: VLAN 10 and VLAN 30 communicate without any impediment through mswitch0. No corrective action is required; the network is operating correctly as configured.
=== END ===